/* Odyssey HUD — the approved interface specification, 2026-09-16.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SHEET IS AND IS NOT ALLOWED TO DO
 *
 * It owns colour, space, radius, borders and state. It owns no type at all: every font size,
 * weight and tracking in the HUD comes from `HudType` through `HudText`, in the Unity-free
 * Odyssey.Hud assembly, because "six sizes and no others" is an acceptance criterion and a size
 * written here could only be tested by parsing this file. There is deliberately not one
 * `font-size` below.
 *
 * Nor does it own the position of a region. Regions are anchored to screen edges or centred by
 * the layout engine, and `HudLayout` is the arithmetic that says where that puts them — which is
 * how "no two panels overlap at 1280x720, 1920x1080 or 2560x1440" became a test in the fast tier
 * rather than an opinion in a review. The offsets below are the anchors themselves (left:20,
 * right:76, bottom:84) and they are the same numbers HudLayout holds; HudStyleSheetTests fails
 * the tier if the two disagree.
 *
 * Colours are literal rather than var(--x), which is the position this sheet has always taken and
 * is worth restating: the sheet stays legible as plain text and there are no custom-property
 * resolution surprises across panel copies. What has changed is that the literals are now checked
 * — HudStyleSheetTests parses the declarations named in its own table and compares them against
 * HudTheme, so a colour nobody agreed to cannot reach the screen.
 *
 * ONE THING IN THE SPEC THIS ENGINE CANNOT DO, AND WHAT WAS DONE ABOUT IT. The panel fill was
 * specified as rgba(12,16,20,.86) plus backdrop-filter: blur(6px). UI Toolkit has no backdrop
 * filter and no way to sample what is behind an element, so the blur is simply absent — and for
 * a while the fill was kept at .86 anyway, on the reasoning that the scrims carried the
 * contrast and HudContrast could prove it. That was true about contrast and wrong about
 * reading: the ratio held while the panel still had a moving, detailed world showing through
 * every letter of it, which is a different complaint and the one the owner made
 * (2026-09-21, of the storage pane: "remove the transparency so we get clarity").
 *
 * The fill is therefore opaque. Translucency without the blur is not the spec at reduced
 * fidelity; it is the half of the effect that costs legibility keeping none of the half that
 * bought depth. HudTheme.PanelFill is the one owner of the value — .panel and .card both read
 * it and HudStyleSheetTests checks them against it, so the alpha cannot be changed in one
 * place only. The nearly-opaque surfaces above it (BarFill .90, PopoverFill .96) are left
 * alone: at 4–10% they never showed the world, which is the thing that was wrong here.
 */

/* Unity's runtime theme gives every Label a small vertical margin of its own. Inside a row of a
   fixed height that costs nothing, which is why it went unnoticed: the stores rows, the clock line
   and the inspect header all size their own children. It shows up on a label that is the last
   thing in a panel — the depth rail's "R / F" hint was adding two pixels to the rail's height, and
   the empty inspect pane's one line would have done the same. Reset first, so that every margin
   below is one this sheet asked for. */
.unity-label {
    margin: 0;
}

.hud {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;

    /* The HUD's ink, inherited by every label that does not name its own.

       This is a fix for a whole class of bug rather than for the one that found it (owner,
       2026-09-17: "I couldn't see any text on the save as/save buttons when saving a world").
       `color` is an inherited property in UI Toolkit, and nothing in this sheet set one above a
       leaf — so a label built without a style class fell through to the imported default runtime
       theme, whose ink is dark, and drew invisibly on a panel that is nearly black. Every label in
       the HUD passes a class and so nobody had been bitten; the moment two did not, they vanished
       silently rather than looking wrong, which is the worst way for a mistake like this to
       present.

       Two labels were already in that state and had been for some time: the naming prompt's
       answers, and "MADE OF" on the Build palette's material row. Setting the inherited ink here
       means the next one is merely unstyled instead of absent. Every class below still overrides
       it, so nothing that already had a colour changes.

       HudStyleSheetTests pins it, because a default nobody can see the absence of is exactly the
       kind that gets deleted as unused. */
    color: #eef3f6;

    /* The shell must not catch the pointer anywhere a region does not sit, or the world would be
       unclickable behind a full-screen element. Set from code (PickingMode.Ignore), where it
       cannot be lost with a stylesheet swap. */
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- scrims

   Always on, both of them, and never a pointer target. They are the reason the panels could
   shrink: a panel dark enough to hold 13 px text over bright terrain has to be nearly opaque, and
   a screen of nearly opaque panels is the coverage this rebuild was asked to halve. The ramp
   itself is a texture built in HudTokens.VerticalRamp, because USS has no gradient property. */

.scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0;
    background-size: 100% 100%;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- panels */

.panel {
    position: absolute;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 1);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 5px;
    padding: 12px;
}

.panel__hdr {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    height: 20px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Every label that shares a column with another declares its height and centres its text in it.
   Left to itself a label claims a line box of about twice the point size — 26 px for 13 px type —
   and two of those stacked inside a 38 px header overlap the tabs under them, which is exactly
   what the first screenshot of this HUD showed. An explicit box with `middle` alignment centres
   the ink inside it, so the leading falls outside the box instead of pushing the next row down. */
.panel__label {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 20px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.panel__hdrright {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- shared bits */

.bar {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-radius: 2px;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bar__fill {
    height: 100%;
    background-color: #7fc98c;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

/* The bar under a need in the inspect pane. */
.bar--need { height: 4px; margin-top: 4px; }

/* An explicit height, because HudLayout.BuildCatRows is a count of these and a row whose height
   is content-driven cannot be counted. 30 px box plus 2 px of margin each side is the 34 px pitch
   the model works in. */
.chip {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 30px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 4px 8px;
    margin: 2px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.chip__label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); }
.chip:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }
.chip--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.chip--off:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }

/* Armed. The accent the rest of the interface uses for "this is on", so a lit palette chip
   and a lit command-bar item say the same thing. */
.chip--on { border-color: #6fd3e3; background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12); }
.chip--on .chip__label { color: #dff6fa; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A1 stores */

/* Docked to the top-left corner (owner, 2026-09-17), so it lines up with the colonist strip
   beside it, which has been at the very top since the bars were docked. The corners that sit on
   a screen edge are squared, for the same reason the command bar's are: a rounded corner against
   the edge shows a notch of world through it. The breathing room moved inside — the panel's own
   12 px padding is untouched, so no text is nearer the edge than it was. */
.stores {
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 168px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
}

.stores__count {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin-right: 6px;
    height: 20px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

.stores__rows { margin-top: 9px; }

.stores__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 29px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0 4px;
}

.stores__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05); }

.stores__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.stores__value {
    color: #eef3f6;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* A commodity the colony has none of. Folded away behind the disclosure by default; at 0.42 it is
   still readable when it is shown, which is what the disclosure is for. */
.stores__row--zero { opacity: 0.42; }

/* Falling: an amber down-chevron before the value, and the faintest tint across the row. */
.stores__row--falling { background-color: rgba(232, 181, 92, 0.09); }
.stores__falling { margin-right: 5px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A2 colonist strip */

/* Docked to the very top (owner, 2026-09-17) and allowed to wrap to a second row. The left and
   right insets are written from code on every resize, because what the strip may occupy is twice
   the smaller of its two clearances rather than the whole screen — and a wrap has to happen at
   that width, not at the screen's. */
.strip {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0;
    top: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

.strip__cards {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    justify-content: center;
    align-content: flex-start;
}

/* Vertical layout: avatar at top with activity badge in bottom-right corner, name centered
   directly below. Narrowed to 96 px so the top bar holds ~30% more colonists per row while
   giving names 80 px of width (a 60% increase over the old 50 px budget). */
.card {
    width: 96px;
    /* 89 until 2026-09-23; the health bar and its gap added 5, which is all the coverage ceiling
       had left (design 33 §9f, HudLayout.CardHeight). */
    height: 94px;
    /* Half the gap on each side horizontally, the whole of it below — so the gap between two
       rows is one CardGap and the first row still starts exactly at the strip's top edge, which
       is what lets the layout model state the strip's height as rows and gaps and be right. */
    margin: 0 3.5px 7px 3.5px;
    padding: 8px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 1);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 5px;
    align-items: center;
}

.card:hover { border-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.55); }
.card--sel { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* The spec's "0 0 0 1px rgba(111,211,227,.35)" outside the accent border. UI Toolkit has no
   box-shadow, so the ring is an element inset past the card's own edge. */
.card__ring {
    position: absolute;
    left: -2px; top: -2px; right: -2px; bottom: -2px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.35);
    border-radius: 6px;
}

.card__avatar-box {
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    align-self: center;
    position: relative;
}

.card__avatar {
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

/* 17x17 px badge docked at the bottom-right of the avatar portrait. */
.card__badge {
    position: absolute;
    right: -2px;
    bottom: -2px;
    width: 17px;
    height: 17px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.90);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 3px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

/* The colonist's own name, placed directly below the portrait and centered. */
.card__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
    height: 19px;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 2px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

/* The health bar (design 33 §9f): always shown, the full width inside the padding, the need bar's
   4px. The fill's width and colour are set from code - the colour is
   CombatFeedbackModel.HealthBarColour, the one owner of the bar over a colonist's head, so no
   colour for the fill is written here. A downed colonist's track is tinted red from code too. */
.card__health {
    height: 4px;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 1px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-radius: 2px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.card__health-fill {
    height: 100%;
    width: 0;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

/* "Downed", across the foot of the portrait: a 4px bar is too thin to carry a word, and the card
   has no pixel of height left to give one a line (HudLayout.CardHeight). The plate is set from
   code in the bar's red; the job badge is added after it and so sits on top at the right. */
.card__downed {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    height: 15px;
    padding-left: 4px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 4px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* Drag and drop slot swapping feedback */
.card--dragging {
    opacity: 0.35;
}

.card--drag-target {
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    scale: 1.04;
}

.card-drag-ghost {
    position: absolute;
    width: 96px;
    height: 89px;
    padding: 8px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.94);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-radius: 5px;
    align-items: center;
    opacity: 0.85;
}

.card-drag-ghost__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
    height: 19px;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 2px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

/* Roster pagination toolbar docked alongside the cards */
.roster-pager {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    height: 24px;
    margin: 0 0 7px 0.5px;
    padding: 0 2px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 1);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.roster-pager__btn {
    width: 18px;
    height: 20px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.roster-pager__btn:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
}

.roster-pager__btn:active {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.20);
}

.roster-pager__label {
    min-width: 32px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    color: #a9b7c0;
    padding: 0 2px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A3/A4 clock and speed,
                                                                     A5 alerts

   One column, not two corners. The clock and the alerts were absolutely positioned with
   hand-picked tops until the clock grew past the 122 px the alerts panel's fixed top allowed it
   and buried the speed buttons. Heights are content's business; only the order is ours. */

/* Docked to the top edge with the strip and the stores panel (owner, 2026-09-17): the clock sat
   twenty pixels below a strip beside it that started at zero, and the two read as misaligned
   because they were. It is not against the right edge — the depth rail is, and this column sits
   inside it — so only its top corners are squared. */
/* Width is HudLayout.ClockWidth said again in the language the panel is actually laid out in.
   Nothing checks that the two agree; change them together. */
.column-right {
    position: absolute;
    right: 56px;
    top: 0;
    width: 271px;
}

.clock.panel {
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
}

.clock { position: relative; width: 100%; }

.clock__line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    height: 28px;
}

.clock__time {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 28px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.clock__date {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 28px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* The outdoor temperature, third on the clock's row. Yellow so a reading that matters is
   findable at a glance without being an alarm - the alert colours are Warn's orange and Bad's
   red, and a temperature that is merely being read must not be mistaken for either. It keeps the
   row's own 28 px so the clock's height, and the coverage budget, are unchanged. */
.clock__temp {
    color: rgba(240, 214, 122, 0.92);
    height: 28px;
    margin-left: 8px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

.speed {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 9px;
    height: 26px;
}

.speed__btn {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    height: 26px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
    border-radius: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.speed__btn:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

.speed__btn--on {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.alerts {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* The negative bottom margin cancels the last row's own gap, so the panel is exactly
   rows * height + (rows - 1) * gap tall — which is what HudLayout models and what the PlayMode
   gate compares the realised box against. The same trick is used by the needs grid and the
   depth rail below. */
.alerts__rows { margin-top: 9px; margin-bottom: -4px; }

/* One line of 13 px type at the renderer's own line height (26 px). Vertically middle-aligned
   icon, highlighted clickable subject, and aligned dismiss button on the right. */
.alert {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    min-height: 26px;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 2px;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

.alert:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
}

.alert__icon {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-right: 6px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.alert__text {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    flex-grow: 1;
    align-items: center;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.alert__target {
}

.alert__prefix,
.alert__message,
.alert__lead {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.alert__detail {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.60);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- toasts (SK4)

   The transient stack, under the alerts in the same column. Design 09 §2.3 calls this a "transient
   toast" and distinguishes it from a bulletin; §3.1 keeps alerts and bulletins apart because
   "merging them produces a system that is wrong for both", and this is the third of the three.

   No header row, unlike .alerts: a heading earns its place over a standing list a player returns
   to, and would be the tallest thing in this stack for most of its life. HudLayout.ToastsHeight
   models it without one. */
.toasts {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* The negative bottom margin cancels the last row's own gap, exactly as .alerts__rows does, so the
   realised box is rows * height + (rows - 1) * gap and matches HudLayout.ToastsHeight. */
.toasts__rows { margin-bottom: -4px; }

/* One line, sized like an alert row so the two stacks read as one column. No dismiss control and no
   justify-content: space-between — there is nothing on the right to push away from. */
.toast {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    min-height: 26px;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 2px;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

.toast:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
}

.toast__icon {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-right: 6px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* The line is three labels, not one: the words before the level, the level, and anything after it
   (owner, 2026-09-21 - the number reads in its own colour). They sit flush against each other, so
   the spaces between the words are the registry sentence's own and none of these may add margin.

   Only the FIRST one shrinks. The number is the thing the owner asked to be able to see, so it is
   the last thing that may be dropped if a long name ever crowds the row - "Constance has reached
   Construction 7" truncating to "Constance has reach... 7" is the right failure, and
   "Constance has reached Construc" is the wrong one. */
.toast__lead {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    margin: 0;
}

/* Colour is set from code (HudTokens.Warn) so this and the passion pip cannot drift apart - the
   declaration here is a placeholder and is never seen, as .skill__fill's is. */
.toast__level {
    white-space: nowrap;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin: 0;
}

.toast__trail {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin: 0;
}

.alert__dismiss {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

.alert__dismiss:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
}

.alert__dismiss:hover > .hud-glyph {
    --glyph-color: #e64a38;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A6 events

   Under the alerts in the same column, built to the same row: an event is a fact that stays
   until dismissed where an alert is a condition that clears itself (design 23 §5), so it is a
   second panel with its own model rather than a fourth kind of alert. Its own class names,
   not .alert reused, because the PlayMode gate counts .alert rows to model the alerts panel
   and an event row wearing that class would be counted twice. */
.bulletins__rows { margin-top: 9px; margin-bottom: -4px; }

.bulletin {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    min-height: 26px;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 2px;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

.bulletin:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
}

.bulletin__icon {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-right: 6px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bulletin__text {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    flex-grow: 1;
    align-items: center;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.bulletin__title {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.bulletin__stamp {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.60);
    white-space: nowrap;
    margin-left: 6px;
}

.bulletin__dismiss {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

.bulletin__dismiss:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
}

.bulletin__dismiss:hover > .hud-glyph {
    --glyph-color: #e64a38;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the right-hand gutter

   One column against the right edge holding the depth rail and, under it, the orders strip
   (owner, 2026-09-17: "a vertical button strip that sits below the depth control … very close to
   the screen border"). A column rather than two anchored panels because the rail is the one
   region the WORLD sizes — its cells shrink to fit the screen, so its height is not a number
   anyone can write down here — and a strip given a top of its own would sit under a rail of one
   particular length and float away from every other. This is the same fix, for the same reason,
   as the clock-and-alerts column: never two panels in one corner with hand-picked tops. */

.column-edge {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 44px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A11 depth rail

   Four pixels of side padding rather than twelve, because the spec asks for 26 px cells in a
   44 px rail and twelve either side would leave twenty. See HudLayout.RailSidePad. */

.rail {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

.rail .panel__hdr {
    height: 14px;
    justify-content: center;
}

.rail__cells {
    margin-top: 6px;
    margin-bottom: -6px;
    align-items: center;
}

.rail__cell {
    width: 26px;
    height: 16px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.rail__cell:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18); }

/* Above the surface is sky, and sky is pale. Below it is rock, and rock is dark. That contrast is
   the one thing a depth readout has to say at a glance. */
.rail__cell--above { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.rail__cell--surface {
    height: 32px;
    background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.25);
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.65);
}

.rail__cell--surface:hover {
    background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.40);
}

.rail__cell--active {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.rail__number {
    color: #0b1116;
    height: 16px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.rail__dot {
    width: 4px;
    height: 4px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    background-color: #e8b55c;
}

.rail__hint {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    height: 14px;
    margin-top: 6px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the orders strip

   The four verbs a player applies to what is already on the board, in the gutter under the rail.
   No label row: "DEPTH" fits 44 px and "ORDERS" does not, and the criteria forbid clipped text —
   every button says what it is in a tooltip instead, as these did when they were 26 px squares in
   the Build palette's header. Six at the top rather than twelve, because a strip with no text in
   it would otherwise spend a quarter of its height on air — and none at the bottom, because the
   last button's own margin is already there. UI Toolkit has no :last-child to cancel it with, and
   a padding that double-counts a gap is how a model and a sheet come to disagree by six pixels.
   See HudLayout.Orders*. */

.orders {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 9px;
    padding-top: 6px;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    align-items: center;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

/* The fill and the border are written from code, in the order's own hue (HudTheme.PinnedActionHue)
   and doubled while it is held — the same treatment, and the same four tokens, the open palette
   wears along its top edge while that order is in the player's hand. */
.ord__btn {
    width: 34px;
    height: 34px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.ord__btn:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* The views strip (design 32 §14): the orders strip's own box and buttons, a gap below it in the
   same gutter. Its buttons are switches, lit while their view is on, in the accent rather than an
   order's hue, because showing the lines is not an order. */
.views { margin-top: 9px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A9/A10 inspect */

/* Docked to the left edge with the stores panel above it. Its bottom sits flush against the command
   bar, so both bottom corners and the top-left corner are squared. */
.inspect {
    left: 0;
    bottom: 50px;
    width: 560px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

/* The tile readout and a selected pile stand in a column, not a band: half the width, with the
   facts in rows instead of a sentence (owner, 2026-09-17 — the same fact is always in the same
   place now, where the joined line made every number hunt for its label). */
.inspect--narrow {
    width: 280px;
}

/* One fact of the readout: the label in a fixed-width column, the value beside it. The label
   column is a width rather than a gap so the values line up whatever the labels are. */
.inspect__rows { margin-top: 2px; }

.inspect__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 20px;
}

.inspect__rowname {
    width: 92px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.inspect__rowvalue {
    flex-grow: 1;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* There is no empty state. With nothing selected the pane is display:none and leaves the layout
   (owner, 2026-09-16); the ".inspect__empty" rule that used to sit here styled a line reading
   "Nothing selected", which is the interface talking about itself. */

.inspect__hdr {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    height: 60px;
}

/* A tile or a pile keeps the shorter header: its slot holds an icon at one of the interface's
   three sizes, not a 60 px photograph of a person. */
.inspect--narrow .inspect__hdr {
    height: 38px;
}

/* No overflow rule: clipping is what the acceptance criteria forbid, and the ellipsis they do
   allow is for a colonist's name on a 132 px card, not for a 560 px pane. */
.inspect__titles {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
}

.inspect__nameline {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 22px;
}

.inspect__title {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 22px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.inspect__meta {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 22px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* Twenty-two and sixteen come to the thirty-eight the layout model gives the header. */
.inspect__state {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 16px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.inspect__actions {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.action {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 26px;
    padding: 0 9px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.action:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }
.action--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.action--off:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }
.action__label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); margin-left: 6px; }

.inspect__close {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.inspect__close:hover { border-color: #e06a5c; }
.inspect__info {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}
.inspect__info:hover { border-color: #7fd0e0; }

.inspect__tabs {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 6px;
    height: 26px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

.tab {
    height: 24px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    border-radius: 3px 3px 0 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.tab--on {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12);
    color: #6fd3e3;
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-bottom-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.tab--off { opacity: 0.42; }

/* Two columns, 9 px between rows and 18 px between columns. The old pane stacked them, which is
   why it was 690 px wide and 302 tall for three numbers. */
/* The colonist body's tab area: one height, whatever tab is showing.

   The pane is docked to its bottom edge and grows upward, so a body that sized itself to its own
   tab moved the header, the tab strip and every row under the pointer each time the player
   changed tab — Needs is two rows of 25 against Skills' seven of 19, ninety-eight pixels apart
   (owner, 2026-09-18: "it resizes every time … it needs to be at least a fixed size"). The
   tallest live tab sets the height here; a shorter one sits at the top of it with the slack
   below, so between tabs only the rows change. HudLayout.InspectTabBody is the same number and
   HudStyleSheetTests holds the two together. */
.inspect__tabbody {
    height: 157px;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

.needs {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;

    /* Eighteen pixels of room borrowed back, so that two 256 px columns and the gap between them
       fit the pane's 534 px of content with the second column's own trailing margin hanging over
       the edge rather than forcing a wrap. Flex counts a trailing margin when it decides whether
       a line has room, which is what made the first version of this grid one column wide. */
    margin-right: -18px;
    margin-bottom: -9px;
}

.need {
    width: 256px;
    margin-bottom: 9px;
    margin-right: 18px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- B1 Skills tab

   The same two-column grid as the needs, for the same reason: thirteen skills in one column
   would make the pane 280 px taller than the screen has to spare over the command bar, and would
   leave half of a 560 px pane empty while doing it. The negative trailing margins are the .needs
   trick above and carry the same explanation. */
.skills {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-right: -18px;
    margin-bottom: -4px;
}

.skill {
    width: 256px;
    height: 19px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    margin-right: 18px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

/* A skill with no system behind it yet. Present and readable rather than hidden — the panel
   catalogue's "disabled with a reason" rule — but visibly not a number you have. */
.skill--off { opacity: 0.45; }

/* The one flexible part of the row. Everything else is a fixed width, so this is what absorbs a
   change to any of them - HudLayout.SkillNameWidth is that arithmetic, and
   HudLayoutTests.TheSkillRowsPartsFitTheRow is what stops this being squeezed until "Construction"
   clips. */
.skill__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.skill__level {
    width: 22px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* Two lozenges rather than the word "major": thirteen rows of words is a column nobody reads,
   and what the player wants from this list is its shape at a glance. */
.skill__passion {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    width: 18px;
    margin-left: 7px;
}

.skill__pip {
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    margin-right: 3px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: #e8b55c;
}

/* The experience bar (SK3). A column of the row, between the name and the level, on the owner's
   first look at it (2026-09-21: "the experience bar needs to sit between the skill label and the
   skill value"). It shipped as a 3px underline absolutely positioned along the row's bottom edge.

   WHAT THE OLD POSITIONING WAS PROTECTING, because it still has to be protected. `.skill` is 19px,
   HudLayout.SkillRow says so, and the colonist pane is one fixed height across every tab; a bar
   stacked UNDER the text would have grown all seven rows and moved the pane's top edge on every
   change of tab, which is the exact thing that fixed height exists to stop. A bar placed BESIDE
   the text costs no height at all as long as it is shorter than the row, which at 6px in a 19px
   row it is. The constraint is kept; only the means changed. Do not read "it is in the flow now"
   as permission to make it taller than the row.

   Fixed width, and the NAME is what flexes. Everything to the bar's right is a fixed width, so a
   fixed bar sits at a fixed offset from the row's right edge and every bar in the grid lines up on
   both edges without anything measuring text. The numbers are HudLayout's - SkillBarWidth,
   SkillBarGap, SkillBarHeight - and TheSkillRowsPartsFitTheRow holds them to the row's width.

   Hidden by default so a row with no simulation behind it needs nothing done to it, which also
   covers the row that is born dead and never changes: the code only touches this when liveness
   changes, and for that row it never does. `display: none` on a flex child takes its width back
   too, so a dead row's name simply runs on through where the bar would have been. */
.skill__track {
    display: none;
    width: 72px;
    height: 6px;
    margin-left: 8px;
    margin-right: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
}

/* Width is set from code, per mille in and per cent out. The colour is the needs' own green
   (HudTokens.Good), set from code so this row and a food bar cannot drift apart - owner,
   2026-09-21: "it should also be using the same green as used for the rest, food bars".

   It was tinted by passion until then, on the argument that the fill was the only place the
   x0.35/x1.0/x1.5 learning rate was visible while it was happening. That argument was wrong about
   which question the bar answers: a player reading this row wants "how far along is she", which is
   the same question a food bar answers and deserves the same colour. The passion is still on the
   row, in the pips, which is where it was always legible.

   The colour declared below is never seen. Every .skill__fill is built in one place and given
   ExperienceInk on the next line, so this is a placeholder rather than the white the bar is drawn
   in - do not "fix" it to a green literal, which would be a second owner for HudTheme.Good. */
.skill__fill {
    height: 6px;
    width: 0;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
}

.need__line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 17px;
}

.need__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 8px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 17px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.need__value {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 17px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

.inspect--tomb { opacity: 0.55; }
.inspect__reason { color: #e8b55c; margin-top: 6px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A8 command bar

   The bar may not wrap and may not overflow. Anything that does not fit goes into Menu, which is
   what lets the inspect pane above it stop guessing at the bar's height — a coupling the old
   sheet carried in a comment admitting it was one. */

/* Docked to the very bottom (owner, 2026-09-17), for the same reason the strip is docked to the
   very top: a bar with a strip of world under it reads as floating rather than as the edge of
   the screen, and the twenty pixels are worth more as board. */
.bar-row {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* The full width of the screen (owner, 2026-09-17), so no corner is rounded and the only edge
   drawn is the top one: three of its four sides are off the screen. It is the edge of the screen
   rather than a panel floating near it, and a popover raised from a button on it has somewhere
   definite to sit. */
.commandbar {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 5px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.90);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 0;
    border-left-width: 0;
    border-right-width: 0;
    border-bottom-width: 0;
}

.cmd {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 38px;
    padding: 0 12px;
    margin-right: 3px;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.cmd:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.cmd__label {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.cmd__key {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
    margin-left: 8px;
}

/* Build, the one item on the row that is a mode rather than a command.

   IT WAS FILLED ALL THE TIME UNTIL 2026-09-17, and that was the bug the owner reported: "the build
   button still stays bold when it shouldn't and is confusing — it's an indicator to whether you
   are truly in build mode". A solid accent fill drawn permanently swamps the .cmd--on wash that
   was supposed to say "open", so the cap looked identical whether the player was placing walls or
   had left the palette three minutes ago.

   So the fill became the *state* and an outline became the resting style: accent border and accent
   ink while nothing is armed, the filled cap the bar always had while build mode is on. Build is
   still the only item on the row with a colour of its own, so it is still as findable as it was —
   what it no longer does is claim to be active when it is not. */
.cmd--primary {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.10);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    padding: 0 14px;
}

.cmd--primary:hover { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.20); }
.cmd__label--primary { color: #6fd3e3; }
.cmd--primary .cmd__key { color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.55); }

/* In build mode: the filled cap. */
.cmd--primary.cmd--on { background-color: #6fd3e3; }
.cmd--primary.cmd--on:hover { background-color: #8adfec; }
.cmd--primary.cmd--on .cmd__label--primary { color: #0b1116; }
.cmd--primary.cmd--on .cmd__key { color: rgba(11, 17, 22, 0.55); }

/* A command with nothing behind it yet. 0.42 is the sheet's one off value, shared with
   .chip--off, .menu__row--off, .action--off and .tab--off; the bar used to sit at 0.55 and read as
   merely quiet rather than unavailable. The hover rule cancels .cmd:hover, the way every other
   off state already cancels its own — a control that lights up under the pointer is promising
   something it will not do. */
.cmd--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.cmd--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }
.cmd--on { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.18); }

.commandbar__divider {
    width: 1px;
    height: 26px;
    margin: 0 3px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- menu popup */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- windows and popovers

   A window is a panel you deliberately opened and are looking at, as against a board panel you
   read while watching the world. One rule for all of them (owner, 2026-09-17): less transparent
   than a board panel, a close X in the top right, and closed by Escape. The board behind a window
   is not being read, and showing it through a list of rows is noise on the one surface the player
   is attending to. */
.window {
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.96);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- The Work tab

   Denser than every other panel, and the one place a panel fill is not the shared token.
   Owner, 2026-09-20: "make this menu less transculent/transparent as it needs to see over menus
   behind a little better." It is the largest panel in the game by a long way and the only one
   with a grid of small coloured cells in it; at .96 the world's greens and the terrain's contrast
   came through the schedule bands and moved them, which is exactly the signal those bands carry.
   The other panels keep .96 because they are small and mostly text. */
.panel.work {
    background-color: rgba(10, 13, 17, 0.995);
}

/* A key entry that is also a button: the schedule half's six blocks. It has to read as pressable
   before it is pressed, which is why the resting state already has a border rather than growing
   one on hover — a control that only looks like a control once you have found it is not one. */
.workkey__btn {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-right: 8px;
    padding: 2px 7px 2px 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.workkey__btn:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
}

/* Armed: this block is the one in your hand, and an hour you click takes it. */
.workkey__btn--on {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.16);
    border-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
}

.workkey__swatch {
    width: 13px;
    height: 13px;
    margin-right: 6px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.workkey__name {
    color: #a9b7c0;
}

.workkey__btn--on .workkey__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
}

/* A window raised from the command bar, which is additionally anchored to the left edge of the
   button that raised it and sits flush on the bar with no gap. The left and bottom are written
   from code, because where a popover sits is a fact about the button that raised it and only the
   laid-out bar knows where that is. Square at the bottom, because that edge meets the bar. */
.popover {
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

.menu {
    width: 268px;
    max-height: 560px;
}

.menu__section {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin: 8px 0 4px 0;
}

.menu__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 29px;
    padding: 0 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.menu__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }
.menu__row--off { opacity: 0.42; }
/* An overlay that is showing (design 32 §9: the power row). The accent at the pressed-chip
   strength, so an overlay left on is visible in the menu that turns it off. */
.menu__row--on { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.14); }
.menu__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

.menu__label {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.menu__key { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A7 build palette

   Three layouts over one set of contents, to `docs/design/17-build-palette-layouts.md`.

   WHAT IS NOT IN HERE. Seven category hues and four material tints, each with four states
   derived from it, is forty-four colour declarations for colours that are already arithmetic on
   a token — so those are set inline from HudTheme by HudShell.Build.cs. That is not a literal
   escaping into the Presentation assembly: the tokens are still the only source, and BuildTier
   is what does the deriving. What is left below is the geometry and the neutral states, which
   are the parts a stylesheet is good at and which HudStyleSheetTests can check.

   WHERE THE PANEL SITS. Docked, not floating. The specification drew all three at a 28px margin
   over a bare board; the real screen has stores down the left edge and the roster strip across
   the top. "Tight and flush to other elements to enable full use of space" (owner, 2026-09-17),
   so Rows and Bar span edge to edge and the bottom is written from code, because what is under
   the panel is the command bar or the inspect pane's collapsed header depending on whether
   anything is selected. */

.bp {
    /* Rows and Bar take their left and right from code; Rail takes a width. Square at the
       bottom, because that edge meets whatever it is docked on. */
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

.bp--rows { width: 372px; }

.bp--rail { width: 840px; }

/* ---- header: BUILD, the mode line, the switcher, ESC, the way out

   One row in all three layouts (owner, 2026-09-17: "shift the toggle view, esc and x onto the same
   row as the build text — this will tidy that up"). It was two stacked lines in Rows, because the
   controls there were eight buttons and with a three-part breadcrumb in front of them the row came
   to 426 px against a 372 px panel. Five of those eight left for the orders strip, and what is
   left fits. */

.bp__hdr {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__hdr-id {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.bp__hdr-ctl {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* The breadcrumb, and the mode line that replaces it while one of the four orders is held.
   Its colour is written from code: cyan for a build, the held order's own hue otherwise.

   It is the part of the header that gives. Rows is 372 px and the longest breadcrumb the content
   table can produce — "Structure > Roof and floor above > Wood" — does not fit beside BUILD and
   five controls at any width this panel has. So the crumb shrinks and ellipsises rather than
   pushing the controls off the end: the same treatment a save's name gets in the load list, and
   the reason the row can be one row at every breadcrumb rather than only at today's. Nothing is
   lost that is not also on the lit tiles directly underneath. */
.bp__crumb {
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    flex-shrink: 1;
}

.bp__mode-icon {
    margin-left: 9px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__cost { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); }

.bp__esc {
    margin-left: 9px;
    margin-right: 9px;
}

/* ---- the layout switcher */

.bp__switch {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__switch-btn {
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-left: 3px;
}

.bp__switch-btn:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

.bp__switch-btn--on {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

/* ---- the four actions, and the way out. Their colours are written from code, because each
   one is a different hue and the hue is what says which mode you are in. */

/* The close button, and nothing else since 2026-09-17: the four order buttons that shared this
   class moved to the orders strip in the right-hand gutter. */
.bp__action {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-left: 6px;
}

.bp__action--close {
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
}

.bp__action--close:hover { border-color: #e06a5c; }

/* ---- bands, which is what Rows is made of */

.bp__body { flex-shrink: 0; }

.bp__band {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__divider {
    height: 1px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    margin-left: 14px;
    margin-right: 14px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ---- a tile, in every tier and every layout */

.bp__tile {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__tile-label {
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* Neutral tier: the sub-types. The categories and the materials paint themselves from code. */
.bp__sub, .bp__sub-tile, .bp__bar-sub {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
}

.bp__sub:hover, .bp__sub-tile:hover, .bp__bar-sub:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
}

.bp__tile--on {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.14);
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.bp__tile--on .bp__tile-label { color: #6fd3e3; }

.bp__tile--off {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
}

.bp__tile--off:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
}

.bp__tile--off .bp__tile-label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

/* ---- 4a, Rows, the default

   A column down the left rather than a band across the screen (owner, 2026-09-17): "short width
   as possible but evenly sized … you could probably fit 4 on a row but increase the height and
   try to use the left hand side of the screen instead of the width". Seven tiles stretched across
   1920 were seven very wide tiles with a small icon adrift in each, and they hid the board the
   player is aiming at. */

/* Four equal columns, wrapping — so seven categories stand two rows deep, which is where the
   height comes from. Percentage widths rather than flex-grow, because a growing row does not
   wrap into even columns: 4 x 23% plus a 6px gap after each fits the band's inner width with a
   few pixels to spare. Same construction as Rail's grids, for the same reason. */
.bp__cats {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

.bp__cat {
    height: 62px;
    width: 23%;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

/* An icon over a label, both centred, so the label's own left margin has to come off. */
.bp__cat .bp__tile-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 4px;
}

.bp__subs { flex-wrap: wrap; }

/* Fixed, and tall enough for the widest category — Structure, at three rows (owner, 2026-09-17:
   "the height needs to stay fixed … so it can accommodate all of the menus"). The panel is docked
   on the command bar and grows upward, so a category with fewer sub-types than the last one does
   not shrink neatly: it drops the whole control down the screen while the player is aiming at it.
   align-content keeps the rows at the top of the reserved box instead of spreading them through
   it. */
.bp--rows .bp__subs {
    height: 144px;
    align-content: flex-start;
    align-items: flex-start;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Reserved for the same reason, one tier down: five of the seven categories open on a tool that
   is not made of anything, and a hidden row takes its height with it. */
.bp--rows .bp__mats-row {
    height: 34px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__sub {
    height: 34px;
    padding: 0 13px 0 11px;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-top: 3px;
    margin-bottom: 3px;
}

/* The material band stacks: the word, the buttons, then the price. Across a full-width band those
   three sat on one line with the cost pushed to the far right; in a 372px column that line does
   not exist, and a 66px label column in front of two buttons would spend a fifth of the width on
   a word. */
.bp__mats {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

/* The palette's captions take the panel-label ink rather than inheriting whatever is above them.
   That is main's fix of 2026-09-17, ported: the old palette's "MADE OF" had no class at all, so it
   took the imported runtime theme's dark ink and had been invisible since the material row was
   built. `.hud` carries a default ink now, which would have covered these — but a caption that
   says what colour it is cannot be uncovered again by the next theme change. */
.bp__mats-label {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
}

.bp__mats-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

/* The same box as a sub-type button beside it (owner, 2026-09-17: "evenly sized in font and size
   as the other buttons but keep the style"). The style is the tint, the doubled border and the
   seated shadow, and all of it survives the box and the type coming back into line — what said
   "terminal choice" was never the extra eight pixels. */
.bp__mat {
    height: 34px;
    padding: 0 13px 0 11px;
    margin-right: 8px;
}

/* ---- 4b, Rail */

.bp__split { flex-direction: row; }

.bp__rail {
    width: 186px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 12px 10px;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
}

.bp__rail-row {
    height: 34px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    border-left-width: 2px;
}

.bp__pane {
    flex-grow: 1;
    padding: 12px 14px;
}

.bp__pane-label {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
}

/* Four columns of tiles, and they are allowed to end in a ragged row rather than close up:
   this layout's whole point is that its height does not move when the category does. The grid
   is sized for the largest category and stays that size, which is the only version of that
   promise the layout keeps by itself — see HudLayout.BuildRailSubRows for the measurement that
   showed it was not being kept. */
.bp__sub-grid, .bp__mat-grid {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-content: flex-start;
}

.bp__sub-grid {
    height: 138px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Reserved even while it is empty. A category whose first buildable tool is not made of anything
   hides these buttons, and a hidden row takes its height with it — which moved the panel by 27px
   and broke the one promise this layout makes. */
.bp__mat-grid {
    height: 92px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__sub-tile {
    height: 66px;
    width: 23%;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

.bp__sub-tile .bp__tile-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 4px;
}

.bp__mat-tile {
    height: 86px;
    width: 23%;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

.bp__mat-tile .bp__material-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 6px;
}

/* ---- 4c, Bar */

.bp__bar-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 8px 14px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__bar-cat {
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 4px;
}

.bp__bar-subs {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    padding-right: 12px;
    margin-right: 12px;
}

.bp__bar-sub {
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 4px;
}

.bp__bar-mats {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.bp__bar-hint { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50); }

/* ---- materials, in every layout

   Two pixels of border where everything else has one, because a material is the panel's terminal
   choice. The fill, the border colour and the ink are written from code: a tinted button always
   means buildable, and an untinted one never does, which is what lets the stock state be read
   without putting a number on the button. */

.bp__material {
    border-width: 2px;
    border-radius: 5px;
}

.bp__material-label {
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.bp__material--on {
    border-bottom-width: 4px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the settings row idiom

   The rows, tabs and columns the old settings panel was built from. The settings window itself
   has its own .sw rules below (design 39); these stay because the debug menu and the start screen
   are built from them. */

/* The debug menu (backtick). Centred exactly like Settings and mutually exclusive with it
   (HudShell.Debug.cs / HudShell.Bar.cs each close the other on open), so reusing the same spot
   costs nothing and needs no second layout to judge. */
.debug {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    /* 280 until 2026-09-24 (owner: "make the debug menu bigger"): wide enough now for the Spawn
       tab's two columns of headed rows (design 33 §9i). */
    width: 460px;
}

.settings__tabs {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 6px;
    height: 26px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

.settings__columns {
    flex-direction: row;
}

.settings__column {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    margin-right: 14px;
}

.settings__column:last-child {
    margin-right: 0;
}

.settings__body { margin-top: 9px; }

.settings__section {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin: 9px 0 4px 0;
}

.settings__column > .settings__section:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
}

.settings__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 29px;
    padding: 0 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.settings__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.settings__label {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* The switch itself: a hollow pip that fills with the accent when the lever is on. A lit chip is
   the on-off idiom the rest of this HUD uses, and the first real UI Toolkit control in the
   project should arrive with an interface pass rather than in a settings panel. */
.settings__pip {
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
}

.settings__row--on .settings__pip {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.settings__note {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* A row that has been pressed once and is asking to be sure. Not named for the exit row any more
   (U38): the settings panel grew Load and Quit-to-main-menu beside it, and the start screen has
   its own Quit, and all of them ask the same question in the same voice. One class, so the answer
   to "what does a row look like when it is waiting for a second press" has one place. */
.row--armed { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12); }

.row--armed .settings__label { color: #6fd3e3; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- B17 the settings window

   One fixed frame, 1240 x 720, centred by fixed offsets rather than by its own size, whichever
   tab is open (design 39). Nothing a tab holds may change it: a short tab leaves space. Flat
   throughout: no radius, no shadow, no gradient. Every colour below is a HudTheme token and
   HudStyleSheetTests holds them to it; the per-tab hues are laid on from C# (SettingsLayout.Hue),
   because which hue is a question about the open tab. The numbers are SettingsLayout's. */

.sw__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    background-color: rgba(6, 10, 12, 0.58);
}

.sw {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    margin-left: -620px;
    margin-top: -360px;
    width: 1240px;
    height: 720px;
    flex-direction: column;
    background-color: rgb(12, 16, 20);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

.sw__header {
    height: 52px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__title {
    margin-left: 10px;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

.sw__close {
    width: 30px;
    height: 30px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__close:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26); }

.sw__body {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: row;
}

/* The rail: the five tabs, then the game's actions pinned to its foot. */
.sw__rail {
    width: 240px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 10px 0;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__tab {
    height: 40px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 16px;
}

.sw__tab:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* The selected tab's inset rail, in its hue. */
.sw__tab-bar {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0;
    width: 3px;
}

.sw__tab-label {
    margin-left: 12px;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

.sw__game {
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 12px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__game-label {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    padding: 0 16px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.sw__game-rule {
    height: 1px;
    margin: 6px 16px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
}

.sw__action {
    height: 38px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 16px;
}

.sw__action:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* Pressed once and asking to be sure: Load, which asks twice. */
.sw__action--armed { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12); }

.sw__action-label {
    margin-left: 12px;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* The content pane: title band, columns, footer. */
.sw__content {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.sw__titleband {
    height: 72px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 24px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__tile {
    width: 38px;
    height: 38px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
}

.sw__titlewords { margin-left: 14px; }

.sw__pagename { color: rgb(238, 243, 246); }

.sw__subtitle {
    margin-top: 6px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
}

.sw__pages {
    flex-grow: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.sw__columns {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: row;
    padding: 20px 24px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* A page with a band across its top (Graphics: Quality) over an inner row of columns, which then
   carries no padding of its own. */
.sw__page {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: 20px 24px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.sw__band {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-bottom: 22px;
}

.sw__columns--inner { padding: 0; }

.sw__column {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin-right: 40px;
}

.sw__column:last-child { margin-right: 0; }

.sw__section { margin-bottom: 22px; }

.sw__heading {
    height: 18px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

.sw__square {
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
}

.sw__heading-label {
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

.sw__heading-rule {
    flex-grow: 1;
    height: 1px;
    margin-left: 9px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__row {
    height: 38px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
}

/* Disabled: the whole row at 45%, and its note says why. */
.sw__row--off { opacity: 0.45; }

.sw__words {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.sw__label {
    flex-shrink: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.sw__note {
    margin-left: 9px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.sw__control {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-grow: 0;
}

/* Segmented: joined buttons, the edges shared by a 1px overlap. The segment after a lit one
   takes the accent on the border it shares with it, which is how the lit segment sits above
   its neighbours without a z-order. */
.sw__segs { flex-direction: row; }

.sw__seg {
    height: 28px;
    padding: 0 11px;
    margin-left: -1px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.sw__seg:first-child { margin-left: 0; }

.sw__seg:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.sw__seg--on {
    background-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
    border-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
    color: rgb(11, 17, 22);
}

.sw__seg--on:hover { background-color: rgb(111, 211, 227); }

.sw__seg--after-on { border-left-color: rgb(111, 211, 227); }

/* Switch: the state in a word, then the track. On is the accent track with the knob right. */
.sw__switch {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.sw__switch-word {
    width: 26px;
    margin-right: 9px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
}

.sw__track {
    width: 38px;
    height: 22px;
    padding: 3px;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    align-items: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
}

.sw__knob {
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
}

.sw__switch--on .sw__switch-word { color: rgb(238, 243, 246); }

.sw__switch--on .sw__track {
    justify-content: flex-end;
    background-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
    border-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
}

.sw__switch--on .sw__knob { background-color: rgb(11, 17, 22); }

/* Slider: a 220 x 4 track with the accent up to the value, a 10 x 18 thumb, the figure after.
   The tracker's end margins are half the thumb, so the thumb's centre sweeps exactly the track
   and sits on the centre mark at unity. */
.sw__slider {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.settings__fader {
    width: 220px;
    height: 18px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__input { overflow: visible; }

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__tracker {
    height: 4px;
    align-self: center;
    border-radius: 0;
    border-width: 0;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    margin-left: 5px;
    margin-right: 5px;
}

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__dragger-border { display: none; }

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__dragger {
    width: 10px;
    height: 18px;
    border-radius: 0;
    border-width: 0;
    background-color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    margin-left: 0;
    top: 50%;
    margin-top: -9px;
}

.sw__fill {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0;
    background-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
}

/* Where unity sits, so putting a fader back the way it shipped is a place (owner, 2026-09-17). */
.settings__fader-notch {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: -3px;
    width: 1px;
    height: 10px;
    margin-left: -0.5px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
}

.settings__value {
    width: 56px;
    margin-left: 14px;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* Select: 190 x 28, the value in mono, a drawn triangle at the right. */
.sw__select {
    width: 190px;
    height: 28px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

.sw__select .unity-base-popup-field__input {
    height: 28px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 0;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.sw__select .unity-base-popup-field__text {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* Key chips: two per action. An unbound slot is an empty chip in dashes (DashedOutline). */
.sw__chips { flex-direction: row; }

.sw__chip {
    min-width: 36px;
    height: 26px;
    padding: 0 8px;
    margin-left: 6px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.sw__chip:first-child { margin-left: 0; }

.sw__chip:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.sw__chip--empty { border-width: 0; }

.sw__chip--listening {
    border-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
    color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
}

/* The footer: the reset button left, the Keys hint right. */
.sw__footer {
    height: 52px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 24px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__button {
    height: 30px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 12px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.sw__button:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26); }

.sw__button-label {
    margin-left: 8px;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

.sw__hint { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); }

/* The focus ring: one for the window, moved to whichever control has the keyboard, drawn last
   so no neighbour covers it. UI Toolkit has no outline. */
.sw__ring {
    position: absolute;
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

/* The leave prompt, over the window's own tokens (design 39 §7): 440 wide, the answer that
   saves in the good ink, the one that does not in the bad. */
#leaveprompt {
    width: 440px;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 0;
}

#leaveprompt .prompt__answer {
    height: 30px;
    border-radius: 0;
}

#leaveprompt .prompt__answer--save {
    border-color: rgb(127, 201, 140);
    background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.12);
    color: rgb(127, 201, 140);
}

#leaveprompt .prompt__answer--leave {
    border-color: rgb(224, 106, 92);
    color: rgb(224, 106, 92);
}

#leaveprompt .prompt__answer--cancel { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the modal scrim

   The third scrim, and the only one that is not always on. The two at the top and bottom of the
   screen are ramps carrying the HUD's text contrast; this one is flat, covers the viewport, and
   says that what is behind it is not available. Flat rather than a ramp, so it needs no generated
   texture and can live here as a plain fill.

   It is a pointer target on purpose, which is the whole of "a modal swallows every pointer event"
   (09-ui-and-input.md section 6, case 5): a full-viewport element under the panel and over
   everything else absorbs what would otherwise reach the world. */

.modal-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    background-color: rgba(8, 11, 14, 0.66);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- B18 the start screen

   The screen before the game: the only one that exists when no world is built. Centred like the
   settings panel, and for the same reason — it belongs to no button, so there is nothing to anchor
   it to. It is a window, so it takes the opaque fill and the close X from .window; the X is
   suppressed on the root screen, which has nothing to close to.

   It introduces no control of its own. A row here is a .settings__row, which is the same row the
   stores panel, the Menu popover and the settings panel are built from. */

/* The title screen (design 40): a dock, full height, flush left, 560 wide at every resolution,
   with the starfield to its right. Flat: the .panel radius and padding are taken back off, and
   the dock carries its own contrast, so the start screen's scrim is clear (it still swallows the
   pointer, which is its job). The numbers are TitleLayout's; HudStyleSheetTests pins them. */
.panel.startscreen {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 560px;
    padding: 0 48px;
    flex-direction: column;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.72);
    border-width: 0;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 0;
}

#start-scrim { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

.title__logo {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.title__mark {
    width: 70px;
    height: 60px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-right: 22px;
}

.title__wordmark {
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    white-space: nowrap;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
}

.title__divider {
    height: 1px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 40px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.title__menu { flex-shrink: 0; }

.title__btn {
    height: 64px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 14px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

.title__btn:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

/* The lit edge, in the button's colour; shown on hover and focus. */
.title__edge {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0;
    width: 3px;
    display: none;
}

.title__btn:hover > .title__edge,
.title__btn:focus > .title__edge { display: flex; }

/* The name and the line under it, as one block centred on the icon. Each line is an explicit box
   with its text centred in it and no margin or padding of its own, because a label left to size
   itself takes a line box twice its point size and the block then sits on the renderer's metrics
   rather than on the icon (owner, 2026-09-24: "all out of whack"). */
.title__words {
    margin-left: 16px;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
}

.title__name {
    height: 24px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
}

.title__name--ink { color: rgb(224, 106, 92); }

.title__desc {
    height: 16px;
    margin: 0;
    margin-top: 6px;
    padding: 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
}

.title__btn--good > .title__edge { background-color: rgb(127, 201, 140); }
.title__btn--good:hover,
.title__btn--good:focus { background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.14); }
.title__btn--good:active { background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.22); }
.title__btn--good:hover .title__name,
.title__btn--good:focus .title__name { color: rgb(127, 201, 140); }

.title__btn--info > .title__edge { background-color: rgb(143, 208, 227); }
.title__btn--info:hover,
.title__btn--info:focus { background-color: rgba(143, 208, 227, 0.14); }
.title__btn--info:active { background-color: rgba(143, 208, 227, 0.22); }
.title__btn--info:hover .title__name,
.title__btn--info:focus .title__name { color: rgb(143, 208, 227); }

.title__btn--violet > .title__edge { background-color: rgb(185, 168, 224); }
.title__btn--violet:hover,
.title__btn--violet:focus { background-color: rgba(185, 168, 224, 0.14); }
.title__btn--violet:active { background-color: rgba(185, 168, 224, 0.22); }
.title__btn--violet:hover .title__name,
.title__btn--violet:focus .title__name { color: rgb(185, 168, 224); }

.title__btn--bad > .title__edge { background-color: rgb(224, 106, 92); }
.title__btn--bad:hover,
.title__btn--bad:focus { background-color: rgba(224, 106, 92, 0.14); }
.title__btn--bad:active { background-color: rgba(224, 106, 92, 0.22); }

/* The keyboard's ring: each button's own, 2 px off it and 2 px thick, shown while it has focus. */
.title__ring {
    position: absolute;
    left: -4px;
    top: -4px;
    right: -4px;
    bottom: -4px;
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: rgb(238, 243, 246);
    display: none;
}

.title__btn:focus > .title__ring { display: flex; }

.title__footer {
    margin-top: auto;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.title__cap { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

/* The load list, in the dock under the divider where the buttons were. */
.startscreen__list {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    min-height: 0;
}

.save {
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    height: 64px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    padding: 0 14px;
}

.save:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* Every line an explicit box with its text centred in it and no margin or padding of its own, so
   the title and the line under it start at one x and the row does not sit on the renderer's line
   box (owner, 2026-09-24: "make sure all the elements are aligned"). */
.save__name {
    height: 24px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Colony, day, date: the day at a fixed width and the date right-aligned, so the two figures stand
   in columns down the list whatever each colony is called. */
.save__line {
    height: 20px;
    margin-top: 4px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.save__meta {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.save__day {
    width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
}

/* A fixed width too, right-aligned inside it: a shorter date ("2 Sep") at its natural width moved
   the day beside it (measured, HudShotTests.PhotographTheTitleScreen). */
.save__when {
    width: 120px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: 12px;
    padding: 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
}

.save--bad { opacity: 0.42; }
.save--bad:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }
.save--bad .save__meta { color: #e8b55c; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the New game screen (U39)

   The seed, a reroll and the row that commits, in the region the load list sits in. It introduces
   no control either: a caption, the .field the naming prompt already restyled, and two
   .settings__row. The only new rule with any weight is the disabled row, which is how the screen
   says that what is in the box does not name a seed. */

/* The word over the box, in the quiet ink every figure in this interface is introduced by. */
.startscreen__seedcap {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 18px;
}

/* Reroll and Start, set off from the field above them by the gap the field itself already keeps. */
.startscreen__seedrows { margin-top: 9px; }

/* A row that cannot be pressed, in the idiom .menu__row--off and .chip--off already use. It is
   drawn rather than hidden, because a Start that vanishes while you are mid-edit is a screen that
   looks broken; faint and inert says "not yet" where absent says "never". */
.settings__row--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.settings__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

/* The debug menu's own inert-row look, identical to .settings__row--off but its own class: that
   one is queried by name in StartScreenTests to find the disabled Start row specifically, and a
   second row sharing it would make that query ambiguous the moment both panels exist at once. */
.debug__row--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.debug__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the menu backdrop

   Behind everything, and only ever seen when there is no colony — with a world running the backdrop
   is the world. The picture and its scale mode are set from code, because the file is loaded from
   Resources and may not be there at all; a clone without the art simply gets the fill. */

.backdrop {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    background-color: #05070a;
}

/* Everything that only means anything with a colony running. One container so it can be put away
   as one thing: half these regions set display in code, and an inline style beats a rule here. */
.worldui {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the setup page

   The first thing in this project that is not a panel. The owner asked for the inspect pane's whole
   skills grid on a candidate; that grid is 900px wide, so three candidates with a detail beside them
   is not something a 420x384 box can hold. New game leaves the box rather than stretching it, and
   the box is left exactly as it is for Load and Settings. docs/design/19-world-setup.md. */

/* A panel, not a new box: the element wears .panel and .window, so the fill (rgba(12,16,20,0.96)),
   the border and the radius are the in-game menus' own tokens and nothing here restates them
   (owner, 2026-09-18: "use the same transparency/translucent as the in game menus, not to
   reinvent"). What .setup overrides is only where it sits and how much air it keeps: inset from
   every edge so the border reads as a box around the interface rather than as a screen border. */
.setup {
    position: absolute;
    left: 24px; top: 24px; right: 24px; bottom: 24px;
    padding: 32px 40px;
}

/* A section heading: "Skills", "Traits". Bigger and bolder than the block under it, and set off
   above so it belongs to what follows rather than floating between two blocks. */
.setup__heading {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    margin-top: 22px;
}

/* Every clickable row on this page carries an outline, so what can be pressed is visible without
   hovering it (owner, 2026-09-18). Scoped to the page: .settings__row is also the settings panel's
   and the Menu popover's row, and those are read over a running world where a grid of outlines
   would be noise. */
.setup .settings__row {
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.setup__title {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
}

/* The board, across the top: what it is called, what number it came from, and how big it is. */
.setup__board {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-end;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
}

.setup__field {
    flex-direction: column;
    width: 220px;
    margin-right: 18px;
}

.setup__size {
    flex-direction: column;
    width: 140px;
    margin-left: 18px;
}

.setup__sizevalue {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 30px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.setup__size:hover .setup__sizevalue { color: #6fd3e3; }

/* A row used inline rather than in a column: Reroll beside the seed, Back and Start along the
   bottom. Same .settings__row underneath, so nothing new is introduced. */
.setup__inline {
    width: 160px;
    flex-grow: 0;
}

.setup__people {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* Two columns rather than however many fit the viewport (owner, 2026-09-18), and set clear of the
   record above it. The grid is a wrapping row in a pane that grows, so at 1920 it had laid thirteen
   skills out four across and filled the page edge to edge; the cap is what holds it to two and
   leaves the right-hand half for whatever comes. Derived in HudLayout.SetupSkillsWidth. */
.skills--setup {
    max-width: 636px;
    margin-top: 24px;
}

/* Wider and taller than the inspect pane's line, at a step up the type scale: this screen is read
   at leisure with no world behind it. */
.skill--setup {
    width: 300px;
    height: 24px;
}

/* The way in, and the only green row in the game. Back and Start sit side by side and read
   identically otherwise, so the colour is what says which is which.

   Written as a descendant so it out-specifies `.setup .settings__row` above, which outlines every
   pressable row on this page: that selector is 0,2,0 and a bare `.setup__commit` is 0,1,0, so the
   grey would have won and the green border would never have drawn. Specificity beats order. */
.setup .setup__commit {
    background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.15);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.45);
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.setup .setup__commit .settings__label { color: #7fc98c; }

.setup .setup__commit:hover { background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.24); }

/* The one being read, beside the three. */
.setup__detail {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 32px;
}

/* The portrait and the record it belongs to, side by side (docs/design/20-avatars.md §3). */
.detail__record {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

.detail__lines {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 18px;
}

.detail__name { color: #eef3f6; height: 26px; }
.detail__trade { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); height: 18px; }
/* A section under the skills now rather than a third line of the record, so it reads as the block
   M7 will fill rather than as another fact about the person (owner, 2026-09-18). */
.detail__traits { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50); height: 18px; }

.setup__footer {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 18px;
}

.colonists__rows { margin-top: 9px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the colonist screen (U40)

   Three candidates, each a name and one line of skills. A card is clicked to keep it through a
   reroll, and a kept one wears .row--armed — the same lit treatment every "this is on" in the
   interface uses, so nothing new has to be learned. */

.colonists {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 340px;
}

/* A row wrapping a column since avatars landed (docs/design/20-avatars.md §3): the face, then the
   three lines about the person whose face it is — name, trade, and what they are good at. The same
   shape a roster card's identity row is.

   Sixty-five is the three lines, and it must not fall under the 60 px face: at 47 the faces ran
   into one another and into the selection outline (HudLayout.ColonistCard). */
.colonist {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 76px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    padding: 8px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
}

.colonist:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* The one whose record is showing on the right. A different question from "kept", which wears
   .row--armed, so it needs a different mark: an outline rather than a fill. */
.colonist--on { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* The two lines beside the face. Overflow hidden so that the name, which is the one text in this
   interface allowed to be cut short, is cut by the column rather than by the screen. */
.colonist__lines {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Dim rather than meta since the skills line arrived under it: three lines at two weights read as
   a hierarchy, and an occupation is flavour where the line below it is the decision. */
.colonist__trade {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 20px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.colonist__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The box a colonist is renamed in, which stands exactly where the name it replaces stood
   (owner, 2026-09-18: "rename your colonist on creation by clicking on the name").

   The .field default is 30px tall with 9px of air above it — right under a caption on the setup
   board, wrong inside a card, where it would push the card past the height its own avatar and
   padding fix. So both are overridden to the name line's own 26, and the card does not move when
   the box appears. The padding comes in because the card is 76px wide and eight pixels of it on
   each side is a third of a name. */
.colonist__namebox {
    height: 26px;
    margin-top: 0;
}

.colonist__namebox .unity-base-text-field__input { padding: 0 4px; }

/* The row that goes back, set apart from the list above it the way the settings panel's exit row
   is set apart from the tabs. */
.startscreen__back {
    margin-top: 4px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
    padding: 0 14px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

/* In the dock its word starts at the save titles' edge, not the row idiom's icon gap. */
.startscreen__back .settings__label { margin-left: 0; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the naming prompt

   Names a save before it is written (owner, 2026-09-17). A modal over a running colony, which is
   the first time that combination exists — the start screen's modal has no world behind it. The
   world keeps running underneath, which is the owner's standing answer for what a modal does. */

.prompt {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    width: 420px;
}

/* The first text field in the project, so it is restyled from Unity's default rather than
   accepted — the default is a pale box with a blue focus ring, which against a near-black panel
   reads as a control from a different program. The scroller and the volume slider went the same
   way; this is the third. No font-size here: type belongs to HudType, and the sheet is checked
   for that. */
.field {
    height: 30px;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

.field .unity-base-text-field__input {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0 8px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    --unity-cursor-color: #6fd3e3;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.field:hover .unity-base-text-field__input { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }
.field:focus .unity-base-text-field__input { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* The two answers, side by side under the field: confirm on the left where the eye already is
   after typing, leave on the right. */
.prompt__answers {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* The answer IS its label, the way a settings rung is — one element carrying the box and the word
   together. It was a box with a Label inside it, and that Label had no class, so it took the
   imported theme's dark ink and could not be seen at all. */
.prompt__answer {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    height: 29px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.prompt__answer:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* Armed: the same accent wash every other row that asks twice uses. It needs its own line because
   .row--armed colours a `.settings__label` child, and this control has no child to colour. */
.prompt__answer--armed {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12);
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    color: #6fd3e3;
}

/* A name that cannot be written — empty, or nothing but punctuation. Disabled with its reason
   rather than a press that silently does nothing, which is the panel catalogue's rule. */
.prompt__answer--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.prompt__answer--off:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }

/* The line under the field: what will happen, in words, before it happens. It is the only warning
   there is, since the write itself is instant and silent. */
.prompt__note {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

.prompt__note--warn { color: #e8b55c; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the drag-select marquee */

/* What the player is holding: one word, in a box whose border is the held order's own colour —
   green for chopping, blue for mining, amber for deconstructing, red for cancelling (owner,
   2026-09-17). Centred over the command bar, out of the way of the board. Never a click target.

   The border is three pixels, and the colour is written from code, because the border is the
   whole of what this says at a glance; the cyan here is the resting value for a build tool, which
   has no order hue of its own. See HudTheme.ArmedBorderWidth.

   The line under the word — "drag over the board · right-click or Esc to stop" — was removed on
   the owner's instruction the same day. It was the only place the right-click gesture was written
   down; the orders strip now lights the button that armed the tool and puts it down when pressed
   again, which is a way out you can see rather than one you have to be told. */
.armed {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% 0;
    bottom: 58px;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 5px 14px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.90);
    border-width: 3px;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-radius: 6px;
}

.armed__what { color: #dff6fa; }


.marquee {
    position: absolute;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
}

/* The bed's owner row: the readout's one pickable fact.

   Two rounds of this were too quiet to find. First a pointer cursor and a hover brighten, on the
   argument that a row which shouted would be a button wearing a row's clothes; then a border and
   a chevron. The owner could not assign a bed either time (2026-09-17, and again after). The
   lesson is that hover is not an affordance — a control has to look like one while the pointer is
   somewhere else entirely — so the value now sits in a filled box with a bed glyph before it and
   its text in the accent colour at full weight. It is a button, and it is dressed as one.

   The box is a plain container on every other row, which is what keeps one row structure for
   facts and for the one control among them. */
.inspect__rowbox {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
}


.inspect__row--pick .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.14);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.45);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding-left: 5px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    flex-grow: 0;
    align-self: center;
}

/* Weight is HudType's and not the sheet's (TheSheetSetsNoTypeAtAll): the value is re-set in the
   heavier Row role when the row becomes pickable, and the sheet keeps colour, space and state. */
.inspect__row--pick .inspect__rowvalue {
    color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
    flex-grow: 0;
    padding-left: 4px;
}

.inspect__row--pick:hover .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.26);
    border-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
}

.inspect__row--pick:hover .inspect__rowvalue { color: rgb(238, 250, 253); }

/* An order's own action dressed as the button it is (owner, 2026-09-23: "colour the cancel button
   a red theme with white text to make it clear it cancels"). Danger is a filled red with white
   ink — #a8352b under white is 6.6:1, where the cancel tool's own #e06a5c would be 3.2 and fail
   small text — and the tool's red is kept for the border so the two read as one colour. Warn is
   taking a laid line up: the remove tool's amber, filled, with the panel's dark ink on it. Both
   come after the pick rules above, which they replace at the same specificity. */
.inspect__row--danger .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgb(168, 53, 43);
    border-color: rgb(224, 106, 92);
}
.inspect__row--danger .inspect__rowvalue,
.inspect__row--danger .inspect__rowchevron { color: rgb(255, 255, 255); }
.inspect__row--danger:hover .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgb(196, 66, 54);
    border-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
}
.inspect__row--danger:hover .inspect__rowvalue,
.inspect__row--danger:hover .inspect__rowchevron { color: rgb(255, 255, 255); }

.inspect__row--warn .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgb(232, 181, 92);
    border-color: rgb(232, 181, 92);
}
.inspect__row--warn .inspect__rowvalue,
.inspect__row--warn .inspect__rowchevron { color: rgb(11, 17, 22); }
.inspect__row--warn:hover .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgb(244, 204, 132);
    border-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
}
.inspect__row--warn:hover .inspect__rowvalue,
.inspect__row--warn:hover .inspect__rowchevron { color: rgb(11, 17, 22); }

.inspect__rowglyph { margin-right: 1px; }

.inspect__rowchevron {
    color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.75);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    padding-left: 4px;
}
.inspect__row--pick:hover .inspect__rowchevron { color: rgb(238, 250, 253); }

/* The picker it raises: one row per colonist plus the release, over the row that raised it. */
.bedowner { width: 220px; }
.bedowner__rows { margin-top: 2px; }
.bedowner__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 24px;
    padding-left: 10px;
}
.bedowner__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05); }
.bedowner__name { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); }

/* The mark column: a tick for whoever sleeps in this bed, a dot for a colonist who has one
   somewhere else, nothing at all for a colonist with no bed. Fixed width so the names below it
   all start at the same x and the column reads down as a list of who is housed. */
.bedowner__mark {
    width: 14px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.34);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}
.bedowner__mark--this { color: rgb(238, 250, 253); }

/* =========================================================================
   Almanac Reference Browser
   Full-bleed, 3-column reference browser (Category Rail, Index, Detail Pane)
   ========================================================================= */

.almanac-panel {
    background-color: rgba(8, 12, 10, 0.96);
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
}

.almanac-hdr {
    height: 54px;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
    justify-content: space-between;
}

.almanac-hdr__title {
    color: #ffffff;
    margin-right: 12px;
}

.almanac-search {
    height: 34px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background-color: rgba(235, 240, 242, 0.6);
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 8px;
}

.almanac-search__input {
    flex-grow: 1;
    height: 100%;
    border-width: 0;
    background-color: transparent;
    color: #000000;
}

/* Unity's TextField draws the typed text in a nested element under the field; the
   outer class alone does not reach it, which left the text invisible (owner, 2026-09-20). */
.almanac-search__input .unity-base-text-field__input,
.almanac-search__input .unity-text-input__field,
.almanac-search__input .unity-base-text-field__input .unity-text-element {
    color: #000000;
}

.almanac-search__hint {
    color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45);
    margin-left: 4px;
}

/* The search box and its reset docked to the box's right edge, one middle element of the
   header so the header's space-between spread is unchanged by the reset appearing. */
.almanac-search-group {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

/* Transparent until hovered, so the panel's chrome stays quiet while the search is
   doing its job. */
.almanac-search-reset {
    width: 28px;
    height: 34px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

.almanac-search-reset:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.almanac-hdr__btn {
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-left: 6px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.almanac-hdr__btn:hover {
    border-color: #7fd0e0;
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.12);
}

.almanac-hdr__btn--off {
    opacity: 0.35;
}

.almanac-hdr__close:hover {
    border-color: #e06a5c;
    background-color: rgba(224, 106, 92, 0.12);
}

.almanac-rail {
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
    padding: 8px 0;
}

.almanac-cat {
    height: 38px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 16px;
}

.almanac-cat:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
}

.almanac-cat--active {
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.14);
    border-left-width: 3px;
    border-left-color: #7fd0e0;
}

.almanac-cat__icon {
    margin-right: 10px;
}

.almanac-cat__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
}

.almanac-cat--active .almanac-cat__name {
    color: #7fd0e0;
}

.almanac-cat__count {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4);
}

.almanac-index {
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
}

.almanac-index__hdr {
    height: 34px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 0 14px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
}

.almanac-index__title {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
}

.almanac-index__sort {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
}

.almanac-idx-row {
    height: 52px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 12px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
}

.almanac-idx-row:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
}

.almanac-idx-row--active {
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.14);
    border-left-width: 3px;
    border-left-color: #7fd0e0;
}

.almanac-idx-row__sq {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 3px;
    margin-right: 10px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.almanac-idx-row__texts {
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.almanac-idx-row__name {
    color: #ffffff;
}

.almanac-idx-row--active .almanac-idx-row__name {
    color: #7fd0e0;
}

.almanac-idx-row__summary {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.almanac-detail {
    padding: 24px 32px;
}

.almanac-band1 {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
}

.almanac-sq96 {
    width: 96px;
    height: 96px;
    border-width: 1.5px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-right: 20px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.almanac-id-col {
    flex-grow: 1;
    max-width: 820px;
}

.almanac-title-line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.almanac-detail__title {
    color: #ffffff;
    margin-right: 12px;
}

.almanac-chip {
    padding: 3px 8px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: #7fd0e0;
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.15);
    color: #7fd0e0;
    border-radius: 3px;
    margin-right: 6px;
}

.almanac-chip--nature {
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3);
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
}

.almanac-definition {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.almanac-live {
    color: #7fd0e0;
}

.almanac-actions-col {
    margin-left: 20px;
    align-items: flex-end;
}

.almanac-action-btn {
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: #7fd0e0;
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.16);
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.almanac-action-btn:hover {
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.28);
}

.almanac-pin-btn {
    padding: 4px 10px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
    border-radius: 4px;
}

/* Picking is off in code as well; this says so in ink. */
.almanac-pin-btn--off {
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    opacity: 0.38;
}

.almanac-grid {
    flex-direction: row;
}

.almanac-sec-label {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
}

.almanac-props-col {
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
    padding-right: 24px;
}

.almanac-prop-row {
    height: 34px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
}

.almanac-prop-key {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
}

.almanac-prop-val {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.almanac-body-col {
    padding-left: 24px;
}

.almanac-body-paragraph {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
    margin-bottom: 16px;
}

.almanac-card {
    padding: 12px 16px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
}

.almanac-card--best {
    border-color: rgba(77, 158, 90, 0.5);
    background-color: rgba(77, 158, 90, 0.08);
}

.almanac-card--avoid {
    border-color: rgba(200, 70, 70, 0.5);
    background-color: rgba(200, 70, 70, 0.08);
}

.almanac-card__title {
    color: #ffffff;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
}

.almanac-card__text {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
}

.almanac-level-row {
    height: 30px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 8px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
}

.almanac-level-badge {
    width: 50px;
    color: #7fd0e0;
}

.almanac-level-name {
    width: 140px;
    color: #ffffff;
}

.almanac-level-note {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.almanac-spec-row {
    padding: 8px 0;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
}

.almanac-spec-hdr {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
    margin-bottom: 2px;
}

.almanac-spec-val {
    color: #ffffff;
}

.almanac-spec-detail {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
}

.almanac-eff-row {
    padding: 8px 0;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
}

.almanac-eff-title {
    color: #7fd0e0;
    margin-bottom: 2px;
}

.almanac-eff-desc {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
}

.almanac-rel-chip {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 6px 12px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    margin-right: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

.almanac-rel-chip:hover {
    border-color: #7fd0e0;
    background-color: rgba(127, 208, 224, 0.14);
}

.almanac-rel-thumb {
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
    border-radius: 2px;
    margin-right: 8px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.almanac-rel-name {
    color: #ffffff;
}

.almanac-rel-reason {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
    margin-left: 6px;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- The context menu (design 33 §7a)

   A small panel at the pointer, raised by a right-click on a thing with more than one answer: a
   weapon's "Equip machete" and "Cancel" today. Its width is set here and never from code, so the
   border-box trap a panel that writes its own width falls into cannot happen (CLAUDE.md); its left
   and top are written from code, because only the pointer knows them. Tighter than a board panel:
   it is a list of two or three rows, not a readout. */
.ctxmenu {
    min-width: 168px;
    padding: 4px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.96);
}

.ctxmenu__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 28px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    border-radius: 3px;
}
.ctxmenu__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }
.ctxmenu__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

/* Cancel sits under a hairline, apart from the orders above it. */
.ctxmenu__row--cancel {
    margin-top: 3px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
}

.ctxmenu__label { color: rgb(238, 243, 246); }
.ctxmenu__row--off .ctxmenu__label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

/* Why a row cannot be chosen, after its words: "Equip machete  Downed". */
.ctxmenu__reason {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin-left: 10px;
}
